    "readout_text": "To determine how many square centimeters of paper Jackson can cover with paint, we must calculate the area of the paint strip he can create using the given dimensions.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the Shape and Dimensions\nJackson's paintbrush creates a **narrow strip**, which is essentially a **rectangle**. The area of a rectangle is given by:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area} = \\text{Length} \\times \\text{Width}\n$$\n\n- **Width** of the strip: 6.5 millimeters  \n- **Length** of the strip: 25 meters\n\nSince the final answer is requested in **square centimeters**, we need to convert both measurements to centimeters.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Convert Units to Centimeters\n- **Width**:  \n  $$\n  6.5 \\, \\text{mm} = \\frac{6.5}{10} = 0.65 \\, \\text{cm}\n  $$\n\n- **Length**:  \n  $$\n  25 \\, \\text{m} = 25 \\times 100 = 2500 \\, \\text{cm}\n  $$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Calculate the Area\n$$\n\\text{Area} = 2500 \\, \\text{cm} \\times 0.65 \\, \\text{cm} = 1625 \\, \\text{cm}^2\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Verify the Result\nTo ensure accuracy, we can cross-check using alternative unit conversions:\n- Convert both dimensions to millimeters:\n  - Width: 6.5 mm\n  - Length: 25 m = 25,000 mm\n  - Area: $25,000 \\times 6.5 = 162,500 \\, \\text{mm}^2$\n  - Convert to square centimeters:  \n    $$\n    \\frac{162,500}{100} = 1625 \\, \\text{cm}^2\n    $$\n\nAll methods yield the same result.\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer\n\n$$\n\\boxed{1625}\n$$\n\n\\confidence{Almost certain}",
